  "name": "divide",
  "description": "Capablanca chess on a board ten wide and eleven tall, cut in two by a wall across the middle row that no piece may stand on, slide through or jump over. The only way across is one pair of portals. One end starts on e3, in White's half, and only White may move it; the other starts on e9, in Black's half, and only Black may move it. Moving a portal is a turn: one step in any direction onto an empty square. White's pieces go in only at White's end and come out one step beyond Black's, in the direction they were travelling; Black's go in only at Black's end and come out beside White's. So the door you move is your way out and also where the enemy arrives, and a piece that has gone through never comes back: to it the far door is a solid block. A pawn reaches its promotion row only by going through. Otherwise it is Capablanca chess.",
